<TopicPage slug="subjects/geography/intermediate/coasts-and-oceans">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Coasts are the busiest interface on Earth: waves, tides, rivers and roughly 40 per cent
    of humanity converge on a strip a few kilometres wide. A coastline is not a line but a
    process — a running balance between <strong>energy delivered by waves and tides,
    sediment supplied by rivers and cliffs, and space for the balance to work itself
    out</strong>. Read any coast this way and it decodes: hard-rock headlands stand forward
    and erode into cliffs, arches and stacks; soft-sediment bays catch the load and grow
    beaches, spits and marshes. This page covers the processes, the landforms they build,
    and the reasons people keep settling the results — plus the ocean-scale machinery
    (currents, El Niño, rising seas) that sets the terms of every local argument.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the coastal machine, from wave energy to rising seas" widget="equationStep" config={coastCfg} />

  <h2>The processes that shape coasts</h2>
  <p>
    Waves are wind energy travelling through water. In deep water each water particle
    circles in place; when a wave enters shallow water its base drags, it slows, steepens
    and finally breaks — converting hundreds of metres of offshore travel into a few metres
    of violent work. Two wave regimes matter. <strong>Constructive waves</strong> (low,
    gently spilling, strong swash) build beaches up; <strong>destructive waves</strong>
    (steep, plunging, strong backwash) tear them down. Which regime you get follows the
    weather: storm seasons switch the coast to demolition. Wave attack erodes rock four
    ways — hydraulic action (air compressed into cracks until rock bursts), abrasion
    (the beach itself hurled at the cliff as grit), attrition (load rounding itself), and
    solution (chemical attack on limestone and chalk). The debris is then sorted and
    redistributed by <strong>longshore drift</strong>: waves usually approach the beach at
    an angle, so the swash runs up obliquely and the backwash runs straight down, walking
    every pebble along the coast in a zig-zag — tens of thousands of cubic metres of sand
    a year on an active shore. Draw the angle in an exercise book and you can predict which
    way any spit will grow: down-drift, always. Tides superimpose a rhythm: the Moon's pull (with the Sun's
    reinforcement at new and full moon, the spring tides) raises and lowers the operating
    floor of every process, and the tidal range — from centimetres in the Mediterranean to
    over 12 metres in the Bay of Fundy — decides whether a coast builds wide mudflats or
    narrow beaches.
  </p>

  <h2>The landforms: erosional and depositional</h2>
  <p>
    Where rock resists, the coast carves. A cliff retreats by under-cutting: wave energy
    concentrated at the base cuts a wave-cut notch, the overhang collapses, and the rubble
    is abraded away, leaving a gently sloping <strong>wave-cut platform</strong> exposed at
    low tide. Where the coast alternates hard and soft rock, differential erosion strands
    the hard rock forward as <strong>headlands</strong> with <strong>bays</strong> between —
    and headlands concentrate wave energy by refraction, which is why they erode fastest.
    Cracks in a headland widen into caves; a cave driven through becomes an <strong>arch</strong>;
    the arch roof falls as a <strong>stack</strong> (Old Harry Rocks on the Dorset coast,
    or the smaller Stumps beside it, are the textbook sequence in progress); the stack
    erodes to a stump. It is a clock as well as scenery: each stage is the same process,
    given more time — which is why a sketch of the sequence earns full marks only when it
    labels the process at work, not just the name of the feature.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where sediment wins, the coast builds. Beaches sort their own load — shingle where
    energy is high, sand where it is low — and self-organise into berms and storm ridges.
    <strong>Longshore drift</strong> constructs the signature landforms: a <strong>spit</strong>
    grows where the coastline turns and the current keeps carrying (Spurn Head at the
    Humber); a spit that grows across a bay traps a lagoon behind a <strong>bar</strong>;
    a ridge that ties an island to the mainland is a <strong>tombolo</strong> (Chesil Beach,
    joining Portland to Dorset with a graded shingle bank — coarse at the exposed end, fine
    at the sheltered one). Behind the beach, dunes colonise by succession, and in sheltered
    estuaries salt marsh builds up through the tide's sorting — mudflats low down, marsh
    and eventually land above the mean high-water line. Every one of these is a sediment
    budget you can read: where supply beats removal, the landform grows; stop the supply
    and it dies.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading a coast: the sediment budget</h2>
  <p>
    Geographers formalise that balance as the <strong>sediment budget</strong> of a
    "sediment cell" — a stretch of coast managed as one system, with inputs (cliff
    erosion, river load, offshore sand moved onshore), transfers (longshore drift, tides)
    and stores (beaches, dunes, spits, the seabed). A worked sketch: the Holderness cell
    gains on the order of 100,000 cubic metres of sediment a year from its eroding glacial
    cliffs — which sounds enormous until you note it all travels down-drift to feed the
    Spurn Head spit and the Humber estuary, so a groyne field or a defended cliff "switches
    off" someone else's supply further along. The same accounting explains why a beach
    vanishes after a marina is built updrift, why nourished beaches must be topped up every
    few years (a transfer, not a fix), and why the only truly permanent coastal defence is
    understanding where the sediment wants to go. Numbers make the argument unarguable —
    which is why modern shoreline plans are written as budgets, not as wall schedules.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> beaches exist to please tourists, and sea walls protect
    them. Beaches are the coast's shock absorbers — a wide beach forces waves to break
    harmlessly; a narrow one lets them hammer the cliff. And walls protect <em>their own
    footprint</em> at the expense of elsewhere: groynes trap longshore drift on the updrift
    side, starving the downdrift coast of the sand that was its defence. The textbook case
    is Mappleton on the Holderness coast, where groynes built in 1991 slowed erosion at the
    village and accelerated it south at Cowden, whose farmland and road now fall into the
    sea. Coastal management is always a redistribution of erosion, never an abolition.
  </p>

  <h2>Opportunities and hazards for people</h2>
  <p>
    The opportunities explain the settlement: coasts concentrate trade (most international
    freight moves by sea through a handful of ports), food (fisheries and increasingly
    aquaculture), energy (offshore wind, tidal power — France's La Rance barrage has run
    since 1966; wave and tidal-stream devices are now grid-connected), tourism, and flat,
    well-watered land at the mouths of the rivers that feed them. The hazards are the same
    geography at high energy. <strong>Erosion</strong>: the Holderness coast in East
    Yorkshire — glacial boulder clay, no beach to defend it — retreats up to a couple of
    metres a year, and has swallowed at least thirty villages since Roman times. <strong>Storm
    surges</strong>: low pressure and gales pile the sea onto the land, and surges ride on
    top of tides and (increasingly) on top of sea-level rise — the North Sea flood of 1953
    killed about 2,500 people in the Netherlands and England and triggered defences such as
    the Thames Barrier (operational 1984), which has closed against surges with growing
    frequency. <strong>Tsunami</strong>: rare but continental in reach, as the 2004 Indian
    Ocean tsunami (around 230,000 deaths) made unforgettably clear.
  </p>
  <p>
    Management is therefore a choice, not a default. Shoreline management plans rank four
    options for each stretch: <strong>hold the line</strong> (defend — justified where a
    city or power station sits behind), <strong>advance</strong> (build seaward — rarely),
    <strong>managed retreat</strong> (allow the coast to move, compensating and relocating —
    the honest answer where defence costs exceed value protected; Happisburgh in Norfolk is
    the emblem), and <strong>do nothing</strong>. Nature-based options increasingly compete
    with concrete: mangrove belts absorb wave energy, trap sediment and even keep pace with
    moderate sea-level rise by building peat — while coastal development has already
    destroyed around half the world's mangroves, and reef and dune systems do the same work
    for free. The 21st-century debate is not "defend or not" but <em>who pays, whose land
    moves, and over what timescale</em> — and, as the sediment budget shows, every answer
    redistributes the problem to a neighbour unless the whole cell is planned together.
  </p>

  <h2>The living coast: reefs, mangroves and salt marsh</h2>
  <p>
    The most effective coasts are built by organisms. <strong>Coral reefs</strong> — the
    largest biological structures on Earth, the Great Barrier Reef runs 2,300 km — grow
    where warm, clear, nutrient-poor water lets the coral animal farm algae in its tissues;
    the reef then breaks waves for the shoreline behind it and hosts perhaps a quarter of
    all marine species. <strong>Mangroves</strong> colonise tropical mudflats with stilted
    roots that trap sediment, absorb up to most of a wave's energy over a few hundred
    metres, store carbon at rates per hectare that rival rainforest, and serve as nurseries
    for offshore fisheries — which is why their clearance for shrimp ponds and resorts is
    an economic decision that usually forgets its own bill. <strong>Salt marshes</strong>
    do the temperate version, and dune systems the sandy one. All three are "blue carbon"
    stores and all three defend better than they are defended: replanting a mangrove belt
    is cheaper per metre than a sea wall and appreciates, while the wall depreciates. The
    modern coast, in short, is an ecology as much as an engineering site.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Zoom out and the coast is an ocean problem. <strong>Currents are climate</strong>: the
    Gulf Stream carries tropical heat to the North Atlantic, warming north-west Europe by
    several degrees for its latitude, as one leg of the global overturning circulation
    (AMOC) — a conveyor that redistributes heat on roughly a thousand-year circuit and whose
    measured slowdown is one of the climate system's monitored tipping points. The
    <strong>El Niño–Southern Oscillation</strong> shows the same coupling at yearly scale: a
    warming of the eastern Pacific that flips rainfall worldwide — Peruvian floods,
    Australian droughts, failed Indian monsoons — and was named by fishers who noticed the
    warm Christmas ("El Niño") current killing their anchoveta. <strong>Sea level itself is
    the slow hazard</strong>: global mean rise is now around 4.5 mm per year and accelerating
    (thermal expansion plus melting ice), with about a billion people living within 10 m of
    present sea level. Meanwhile the ocean is absorbing both heat and our carbon dioxide —
    surface pH has dropped about 0.1 since pre-industrial times, a 30 per cent increase in
    acidity that corrodes the carbonate chemistry reefs and shellfish depend on. And the
    map is still blank: oceans cover 71 per cent of the planet, but over three-quarters of
    the seabed is unmapped at modern resolution — the Seabed 2030 project exists because we
    hold better topography of Mars than of our own abyssal plains. The coast, in short, is
    where the planet's largest machine touches the people; the processes are ancient, and
    the stakes are new.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Haslett, Coastal Systems; NOAA ocean data; IPCC Special Report on the Ocean and Cryosphere (2019); Masselink & Hughes, Introduction to Coastal Processes and Geomorphology.</p>
